\documentclass{article}
\title{Open-Vocabulary Object Detection with Region-Text Alignment}
\author{Anon}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
Object detection has been studied for decades, and modern detectors achieve remarkable accuracy on closed-set benchmarks where every test category appears in the training data. However, real-world deployments constantly encounter novel categories, and collecting annotations for every possible category is prohibitively expensive, so open-vocabulary detection has emerged as an important research direction that aims to detect objects from categories never seen during training by leveraging vision-language pretraining. Existing approaches typically distill knowledge from a pretrained vision-language model into a detector, but they suffer from two problems: the region features are misaligned with the text space, and background proposals overwhelm the training signal. In this paper we make three contributions. First, we propose a region-text alignment module that projects region features into the text embedding space with a lightweight adapter, closing the modality gap. Second, we design a background-aware reweighting scheme that down-weights unlabeled proposals, which stabilizes training. Third, we introduce a benchmark protocol with standardized splits for fair comparison. Extensive experiments on multiple datasets show that our method outperforms prior work by a clear margin, and ablation studies confirm that each component contributes to the final performance.
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
Open-vocabulary detection extends detectors to novel categories.
\end{document}
